course: books
lesson: 34
title: The Wind in the Willows, chapter III, part 2: The Wild Wood (the faces, the whistling, the pattering — and the Rat sets out)
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: Dusk falls fast. Then the faces begin: a little evil wedge-shaped face in a hole, gone when the Mole turns; then another; then it seems every hole has a hard-eyed face glaring at him. He leaves the path to get away from the holes.
explain: Then the whistling begins — faint and shrill, behind him, then ahead, then on both sides, passed along through the whole wood. They are awake and ready, whoever they are, and he is alone and far from help.
explain: Then the pattering begins: little feet, closing in from every side like hail. A rabbit dashes past him: «Get out of this, you fool, get out!» The Mole panics, runs blindly, falls over things, and at last crawls into the hollow of an old beech tree, trembling. Now he knows the Terror of the Wild Wood, the thing the Rat tried to protect him from.
explain: Back home, a coal slips in the fire and the Rat wakes up. The Mole is gone. His cap is missing from its peg and his new goloshes from beside the umbrella-stand. Outside, the fresh tracks in the mud lead straight to the Wild Wood.
explain: The Rat looks very grave, and stands «in deep thought for a minute or two». Then he straps on a belt, pushes two pistols into it, takes a big stick, and sets off for the Wild Wood at a smart pace.
fact: «Then the faces began.» — «a little evil wedge-shaped face, looking out at him from a hole. When he turned and confronted it, the thing had vanished.»
fact: He told himself «not to begin imagining things, or there would be simply no end to it»; then «every hole, far and near, and there were hundreds of them, seemed to possess its face ... all hard-eyed and evil and sharp».
fact: «Then the whistling began.» — first «far behind him», then «far ahead of him», then «on either side», passed along the whole wood. «And he—he was alone, and unarmed, and far from any help; and the night was closing in.»
fact: «Then the pattering began.» He thought at first it was falling leaves, but «he knew it for nothing else but the pat-pat-pat of little feet».
fact: A rabbit «almost brushed him as it dashed past, his face set and hard, his eyes staring. "Get out of this, you fool, get out!"», and dived down «a friendly burrow».
fact: «The pattering increased till it sounded like sudden hail on the dry leaf-carpet»; the wood seemed to be «hunting, chasing, closing in round something or—somebody?»
fact: «In panic, he began to run too, aimlessly, he knew not whither» (whither = where to); he «took refuge in the deep dark hollow of an old beech tree».
fact: «he knew it at last, in all its fullness ... that thing which the Rat had vainly tried to shield him from—the Terror of the Wild Wood!» (vainly = without success).
fact: Meanwhile the Rat dozed, «his paper of half-finished verses slipped from his knee ... and he wandered by the verdant banks of dream-rivers» (verdant = green).
fact: «Then a coal slipped, the fire crackled and sent up a spurt of flame, and he woke with a start»; he wanted to ask the Mole «if he knew a good rhyme for something or other».
fact: He called «Moly!» several times. «The Mole's cap was missing from its accustomed peg. His goloshes, which always lay by the umbrella-stand, were also gone.» (goloshes = rubber overshoes for mud and snow).
fact: «The goloshes were new, just bought for the winter, and the pimples on their soles were fresh and sharp» — so the tracks were easy to read, «leading direct to the Wild Wood».
fact: «he re-entered the house, strapped a belt round his waist, shoved a brace of pistols into it, took up a stout cudgel» (a brace = a pair; a cudgel = a thick heavy stick). In this old story the Rat carries them to scare off enemies.
fact: Old words: «confronted» = turned to face; «braced himself up» = got ready to be brave; «hearken» = listen; «slacken pace» = slow down; «grave» = serious and worried; «at a smart pace» = quickly.
fact: Talk in this lesson: «bricking it» (UK) = very scared; «sketchy» = unsafe, dodgy; «yer man» (Ireland) = that fella; «lah» (Singapore) = a word added for feeling, like «you know»; «can or not» (Singapore) = is it possible?; «tara-a-bit» (Black Country) = see you later.
exercise: What begins first in the wood? | The faces | The whistling | The pattering | The falling snow
exercise: What is the first face like? | Little, evil and wedge-shaped | Round and friendly, with whiskers and a twinkle in its eye | Big and stripy | Sad
exercise: What happens when the Mole turns to face it? | It vanishes | It smiles and says «Hullo, Mole!» | It bites him | It grows bigger
exercise: What does the Mole tell himself? | Not to begin imagining things | To go home at once and tell the Rat everything | To shout for help | To fight
exercise: Why does the Mole leave the path? | To get away from the holes | He sees Badger's door through the trees and runs to it | He's lost | He's chasing a rabbit
exercise: What comes after the faces? | The whistling | The pattering of feet | The snow falling | A motor-car hooting
exercise: Where does the whistling seem to come from? | Behind, then ahead, then on both sides | Only from the Rat's house far away across the fields | The sky | A bird
exercise: «he was alone, and unarmed, and far from any help». What does «unarmed» mean? | Without anything to defend himself | Without any arms on his body, only paws and legs | Without a map | Without a coat
exercise: What does the Mole first think the pattering is? | Falling leaves | Raindrops falling | The Rat coming to find him with his stick | Hail on the branches
exercise: What does the rabbit say as it dashes past? | «Get out of this, you fool, get out!» | «Sixpence, please» | «Follow me!» | «O my! O my! O my!»
exercise: Where does the rabbit go? | Down a friendly burrow | Up the nearest tall tree | Out of the wood altogether | Into the Mole's beech hollow
exercise: What does the pattering end up sounding like? | Sudden hail on dry leaves | Gentle summer rain falling on the river by the Rat's house | Drums | Clapping
exercise: What does the Mole do in his panic? | Runs, falls, darts and dodges | Stands still and shouts for Badger by name | Climbs a tree | Digs a tunnel
exercise: Where does the Mole hide at last? | The hollow of an old beech tree | Down a rabbit-hole, with the rabbit | Under a log | In a hedge
exercise: What does the Mole finally know «in all its fullness»? | The Terror of the Wild Wood | The way out | Badger's address | The Rat's poem
exercise: What is the Rat dreaming of? | The green banks of dream-rivers | Motor-cars racing along a dusty high-road with Toad at the wheel | Weasels | Toad's caravan
exercise: What wakes the Rat? | A coal slipping in the fire | The Mole shouting from far away | The doorbell | The cold
exercise: Why does the Rat look for the Mole? | To ask him for a rhyme | To make the tea and toast hot buttered crumpets by the fire | To go boating | To read him a story
exercise: What is missing from the hall? | The Mole's cap and goloshes | The Rat's boat and oars | The umbrella | The cudgel
exercise: Why are the tracks easy to follow? | The new goloshes leave sharp prints | The Mole dragged a long stick behind him all the way through the mud | The Mole dropped crumbs | It was snowing
exercise: Where do the tracks lead? | Straight to the Wild Wood | To Toad Hall | To the river | Round in circles
exercise: What does the Rat take with him? | A belt, two pistols and a cudgel | A lantern, a map of the wood, some rope and a packet of sandwiches | Nothing | His boat
exercise: «a brace of pistols». What does «brace» mean here? | A pair | A leather belt | A wooden box | Many of them
test: Ellie, proper bricking it (very scared) reading this: «the Mole saw a face, so he walked right up and said hello.» Put it right. | He turned to face it and it vanished | Right: he said hello and the face said hello back politely | He ran home | He hit it
test: Why is it so scary that the faces vanish when the Mole turns? | He can never be sure they're real | They are friendly but very shy, so they hide whenever anyone looks | They come back as birds | They're painted on
test: The writer says «Then the faces began.» — «Then the whistling began.» — «Then the pattering began.» Why repeat «Then ... began»? | Each new fear piles on, like steps to a climax | The writer forgot he had written it already | To fill the page | It's a song
test: Tyler, bit sketchy about it: «the rabbit came to help the Mole, yer man (that fella) was dead kind.» Is he right? | No: he dashed past and only shouted «get out!» | Yes: he took the Mole down his burrow and gave him tea | No: he bit the Mole | Yes: he led him home
test: The whistling seems «caught up and passed on throughout the whole length of the wood». What does that suggest? | Many creatures signalling to each other | Just one bird singing as it flies from tree to tree all round the wood | The wind | The Rat
test: Why does the Mole run «aimlessly»? | Panic: he has no idea which way is safe | He knows a quick shortcut to Badger's house and wants to get there before dark | He's playing | He's chasing the faces
test: What would have happened if the Mole had stayed on the path? | He'd still have been near the holes | He'd have got safely home by tea-time, as paths always lead out of woods | He'd have met Toad | Nothing
test: Looking back: in chapter I, what did the Rat say «you can't really trust» — and does this part prove it? | Weasels, stoats and foxes — and yes, he was right | Squirrels — and no, they are kind | Otters — yes | Rabbits — no
test: Looking back: what did the Rat «vainly» try to shield the Mole from, according to the book? | The Terror of the Wild Wood | Toad's new motor-car and his dangerous driving on the roads | The cold | A party
test: Looking back: when the Mole first fell in the river (chapter I), who saved him — and who is coming now? | The Rat both times | The Otter, then Badger | Toad first, then the Rat | Nobody came either time
test: Looking back: in chapter I the Rat «laughed» as he rescued the Mole. How is his mood different now? | He looks very grave — this danger is real | He laughs again, just as in the river, as nothing really matters | He is angry | He's sleepy
test: Looking back: the Rat «hated disappointing people» in chapter II. What does he do for his friend here? | Goes into the dangerous wood at once to find him | Waits by the fire for the Mole to come home by himself | Sends Toad | Writes a poem
test: What does the Rat do before deciding? | Stands «in deep thought for a minute or two» | Runs off at once without thinking at all | Calls the police | Goes to bed
test: Why does the Rat take weapons this time, when he goes nowhere else armed? | The Wild Wood is dangerous for small animals | He wants to go hunting for supper in the wood | Toad told him | He always does
test: ★★★ «a little narrow face, with hard eyes». The book never says whose faces they are. From chapter I, what's the best guess? | Weasels and stoats | Rabbits, who are a mixed lot, as the Rat said in chapter I | Squirrels and hedgehogs | Badgers and field-mice
test: ★★★ The Mole thinks: «yes!—no!—yes!» when he sees the second face. What is the writer showing? | He's not sure if he saw it, then he is sure | He is talking to the face | He agrees with the Rat | He's counting
test: ★★★ The goloshes were «new, just bought for the winter». Why does the writer give us that detail? | It explains the sharp, easy tracks | It tells us the Mole had plenty of money to spend on new things for the winter | It's about shopping | They're magic
exam: Identify the three successive phenomena which assailed the Mole within the Wild Wood. | Faces, whistling and pattering | Snow, wind and rain of exceptional severity | Rabbits, foxes and owls | Three motor-cars
exam: Ascertain the place of refuge selected by the Mole. | The hollow of an old beech tree | A rabbit's burrow | The Badger's front door | A disused mill
exam: Select the response which most accurately characterises the evidence by which the Rat determined his friend's direction. | Fresh golosh prints leading to the Wood | A note left upon the parlour table stating the Mole's intended destination | A rabbit's testimony | Whistling
exam: It is given that the Rat departed in search of the Mole. Identify the articles with which he equipped himself. | A belt, a brace of pistols and a stout cudgel | A lantern and a rope | A boat-hook and a net | Nothing whatsoever
